# Canonical Ruby reference and tag recapture

Status: accepted DEC102 reference, applied to DECKO-818.

The project's reference is the existing VM's Ruby 3.2.3 and card 1.110.0.
Ruby reference, oracle and comparison executions use that VM. Earlier Windows
Ruby 3.3/3.4 captures retain their historical provenance; they do not redefine
the current reference. This is a reference correction, not permission to
relax a comparison or change expected bytes without native observations.

The shared tag-wrapper fixture is recaptured through
`tests/support/recapture-tag-wrapper-vm.mjs`. Its adjacent VM provenance file
binds the full native response, Ruby/gem versions, original probe and source
hashes. The same pinned Ruby methods execute; the fixture changes only the
observed runtime and the reference's malformed-wrapper Hash spelling.
Numeric runtime revision expectations now bind the actual 52bb2ac0a6 revision.

The recapture exposed thirteen exact product differences: twelve portable
Hash-rendering cases and one malformed-wrapper diagnostic. The raw differences
were retained before product changes in the DECKO-818 evidence. Ruby 3.2 emits
`{:opts=>:x}` where the previous implementation emitted Ruby 3.4's
`{opts: :x}`. Nested tag values and wrapper diagnostics now use the canonical
Symbol and Hash spelling. Every original assertion and comparison stays exact.

Native observations record 280 symbol/hash spellings, including operators,
variables, Unicode and quoted names, with source and stdin hashes. The initial
59 checks fail on the previous implementation. An independent 221-input
recapture then found six additional operator/global-name differences; those
failed before the correction and now retain their exact native expectations.
Original probe scope remains
explicit: selected original Decko/ActionView methods with disclosed synthetic
format backing, not a complete Rails application or whole-port acceptance.

Stored-capture comparisons may execute locally without Ruby. Their provenance
must bind the genuine VM recapture. No Windows Ruby exception, interpreter
installation, shared VM application change or private-corpus substitution is
part of this correction.
